MAME Newport: publish each finished frame into a shared-memory framebuffer.

WHY

The SGI IRIX gallery exhibit captures MAME's output and streams it to a browser.
Doing that through a WINDOW costs a second software rasterisation of every
frame: MAME rasterises the Newport framebuffer into an RGB32 bitmap, uploads it
to an SDL texture, SDL_RenderCopy's that through Mesa llvmpipe into an X window,
and the capture daemon reads the same pixels back out again. A 1280x1024x4 frame
is software-rasterised twice in series and pushed through X in between, and it
measured 226-257 Gcyc -- ~1.5-1.7 Gcyc per emulated second, 32-43% of host time.

Every cheap fix was measured dead before this one was written:
`SDL_RENDER_DRIVER=software` changes nothing (so llvmpipe was never the cost --
it is raw pixel movement on a memory-bound path), `-nofilter` is worth ~1.8%, a
bigger unscaled Xvfb is worse, and throttling is neutral.

Publishing the finished frame here lets MAME run `-video none` -- no window, no
X server at all -- and hands the consumer the EXACT emulated framebuffer instead
of a window-scaled resample. That last part is a fidelity fix as much as a speed
one: MAME's X window was 1272x954 on a 1280x1024 Xvfb and auto-scaled with the
display, while the emulated Newport framebuffer is 1288x1024 once IRIX programs
the VC2 (about 85 frames into boot).

MEASURED (interleaved A/B, both arms off ONE binary since this is env-gated, so
the only difference is the display path; cycle-normalised emulated_seconds /
(cycles/2.5e9) over the emulated 150-300 s window, attributed within each run):

  -video soft on an Xvfb   754.8 / 780.4 Gcyc   49.7% / 48.1% @2.5GHz
  -video none + this       535.0 / 547.1 Gcyc   70.1% / 68.5% @2.5GHz
  -video none, publisher off 509.0 / 520.9 Gcyc 73.7% / 72.0% @2.5GHz

+41.0% and +42.4% within-round against the windowed path, with the publisher
itself costing a consistent 4.9% against the no-display ceiling -- i.e. ~85% of
the maximum available win. The 226 Gcyc saved over the 150 s window is 1.51 Gcyc
per emulated second, matching the independently profiled cost of the display
path.

DESIGN

Env-gated on IRIX_SHM_PATH: with the variable unset the first statement of
publish_shm() returns, so this is completely inert for every other use of MAME.

Wire format is a 64-byte header (magic 'IFB1', version, width, height, stride,
bpp, u64 sequence, dirty rect) followed by width*height RGB32 pixels. RGB32 is
host-endian XRGB8888, i.e. B,G,R,X in memory on x86, which is byte-identical to
what a BGRA consumer expects -- there is no per-pixel conversion anywhere on the
path, and one would eat the entire win.

Synchronisation is a seqlock: the sequence goes ODD before the pixels are
touched and EVEN after, both with release ordering, and a reader takes it with
acquire, copies, re-reads, and retries on odd-or-changed. One producer, any
number of readers, readers never write. Validated under maximum write pressure:
200 accepted samples, 3,005 torn reads correctly rejected, every accepted sample
internally self-consistent.

Damage comes free. The frame is published from BOTH paths of screen_update() --
the cache hit and the fresh render -- and the dirty flag is the one the
whole-frame cache (mame-newport-dirty-frame-cache.patch) already maintains, so
an unchanged frame is published with an EMPTY dirty rect and a consumer can skip
it exactly as XDamage would. What the header does not carry is sub-frame
granularity; a consumer that wants a real bbox diffs successive frames itself.

DEPENDS ON mame-newport-dirty-frame-cache.patch (it uses that patch's dirty
flag and publishes from its cache-hit path). Apply it after that one.

The consumer is streamhost's `SH_CAPTURE=shm` backend,
streamhost/streamhost/src/capture/shm.rs.

